// Innovation data — patents & papers.
// Each entry is rendered as a slim, single-line row; full details show in a modal.
//
// Schema:
//   type          "patent" | "paper"
//   title         Full title (required)
//   subtitle      Short tagline shown under the title in the modal (optional)
//   venue         Patent office / conference / journal name
//   date          Human-readable date shown on the card and modal
//   sortDate      YYYY-MM-DD for sorting (newest first)
//   identifier    Patent number / DOI / arXiv id / etc. (optional)
//   authors       "A, B, C" — for papers (optional)
//   summary       Plain-English explainer — shown by default in the modal (recommended)
//   abstract      Original technical abstract — shown when reader flips the toggle (optional)
//                 If BOTH `summary` and `abstract` are present, the modal shows a segmented
//                 toggle: [In Plain English | Technical Abstract], defaulting to Plain English.
//                 If ONLY one is present, that one is shown alone without a toggle.
//   tags          ["AI", "Search", ...] (optional)
//   primaryLink   { label, url, icon } — prominent button at the top of the modal (optional)
//   links         [ { label, url, icon } ] — extra links shown lower in the modal (optional)
//
//   ---- Patent-only ----
//   status        "granted" | "filed" | "under-review"  — shown as the colored pill on the card
//
//   ---- Paper-only ----
//   format        Free-text label; commonly one of:
//                     "Long Paper" | "Short Paper" | "Workshop Paper" | "Tutorial"
//                     | "Extended Abstract" | "Poster" | "Talk" | "Journal Article"
//                     | "Technical Report" | "Demo"
//                 Rendered as the colored pill on the card (replaces the old "Published" status).
//                 Unknown values still render with a neutral slate pill.
//   archival      true (default) | false  — shows an unobtrusive badge in the modal header.
[
    // ---------------------------- PATENTS ----------------------------
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "granted",
        "title": "Generating Narrative Query Responses Utilizing Generative Language Models from Search-Based Autosuggest Queries",
        "subtitle": "A query gateway for context-retained, in-flow AI chat from search autosuggest",
        "venue": "US Patent Office",
        "date": "Issued Dec 12, 2024",
        "sortDate": "2024-12-12",
        "identifier": "US20240411824A1",
        "summary": "A query gateway system that provides an efficient framework for context-retained autosuggest queries from search engines to generative language models, enabling seamless AI chat experiences without disrupting user navigation. The system bridges the traditional search box with a conversational AI response layer so users can pivot from a typed lookup to a rich, narrative answer in a single interaction.",
        "abstract": "This disclosure describes a query gateway system that provides an efficient and flexible framework for providing context-retained autosuggest queries from an autosuggest query system (e.g., a search engine query experience) to a generative language model system (e.g., an AI chat experience). For instance, the query gateway system establishes a framework to leverage the features and services of the autosuggest query system and automatically provides context-retained queries to the generative language model system using separate user interfaces that do not disrupt user navigation or require manual duplicative user input. Additionally, the query gateway system incorporates additional enhancements, including an AI chat eligibility model and a query reformulation model, to improve the computational efficiency and accuracy of the AI chat system.",
        "tags": ["Generative AI", "Search", "LLM", "Microsoft Bing"],
        "primaryLink": {
            "label": "View on Google Patents",
            "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/US20240411824A1/",
            "icon": "fas fa-external-link-alt"
        }
    },
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "filed",
        "title": "Dynamic Generation and Personalization of Starter Prompts for AI Agents Based on Capability Discovery and User Context",
        "subtitle": "Personalized \"what should I ask?\" suggestions that evolve with an agent's tools and your workflow",
        "venue": "Filed at USPTO",
        "date": "Filed Apr 21, 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-04-21",
        "summary": "When you open an AI agent, it usually greets you with a few \"starter prompt\" suggestions. This system makes those suggestions actually useful — it continuously watches what the agent can do (its tools and connected data sources), combines that with what it knows about you (your role, past interactions, work context), and uses a language model to generate personalized suggestions on the fly. As the agent gains new capabilities or your workflow shifts, the starter prompts stay fresh and relevant instead of being generic boilerplate.",
        "abstract": "A system monitors, via a capability monitoring service, capabilities of an artificial intelligence agent including tools and resources available through one or more tool providers. The system detects a change in the capabilities and receives enterprise knowledge metadata associated with a user of the agent via one or more enterprise knowledge application programming interfaces. The system obtains user behavioral signals representing prior interactions of the user with the agent. The system generates, using a language model and based on the capabilities, the enterprise knowledge metadata, and the user behavioral signals, a set of candidate starter prompts for the agent. The system causes presentation, via a user interface of the agent, of a subset of the starter prompts to the user.",
        "tags": ["AI Agents", "Personalization", "Copilot", "UX"]
    },
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "filed",
        "title": "Cross-Platform Memory Portability for Conversational Artificial Intelligence Platforms",
        "subtitle": "Take your AI's memory of you with you — securely, across platforms",
        "venue": "Filed at USPTO",
        "date": "Filed Mar 9, 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-03-09",
        "summary": "Today, every AI assistant — ChatGPT, Copilot, Claude, Gemini — keeps its own private memory of your preferences, past conversations, and custom instructions, and there's no way to take that memory with you when you switch platforms. This system defines a universal, secure format for exporting an AI's memory from one platform and importing it into another. It converts platform-specific data into plain-language entries, uses a dual-layer encryption scheme so your personal content is unlocked only by your password (while platform-proprietary bits stay platform-locked), and shows a preview + confirmation step before anything moves. The result: your AI genuinely knows you regardless of which product you're using.",
        "abstract": "A cross-platform memory portability architecture enables secure export and import of conversational artificial intelligence memory data across heterogeneous platforms. Memory data comprising conversational memories and custom instructions is retrieved from a source platform via a platform-specific adapter and transformed into a universal memory schema format that is platform-agnostic. A transformation engine converts platform-specific structured fields into natural-language entries while enforcing non-augmentation constraints to prevent addition of inferred content. The transformed memory data is serialized into an export file that may be encrypted using a dual-layer encryption architecture, wherein user-generated content is encrypted with a password-derived key for cross-platform portability and platform-proprietary data is separately encrypted with a platform-managed key for same-platform restoration. Upon import, the export file is validated, decrypted if necessary, transformed into a target platform-specific format, and ingested into the target platform. Preview and confirmation mechanisms provide user transparency and control throughout the portability workflow.",
        "tags": ["AI Memory", "Portability", "Privacy", "Encryption", "Conversational AI"]
    },
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "filed",
        "title": "Profile-Based Memory Architecture for an AI/ML Service",
        "subtitle": "Multi-persona AI with clean contextual boundaries",
        "venue": "Filed at USPTO",
        "date": "Filed Jan 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-01-15",
        "summary": "A multi-profile memory system that lets a single AI assistant maintain independently scoped profiles — Work, Personal, Project-specific, or Incognito — each with its own stored preferences, tone, and history. The system intelligently detects context shifts, proactively recommends profile switches, and enforces clean information boundaries, enabling personalized, privacy-preserving AI without manual resets or separate accounts.",
        "tags": ["AI Personalization", "Memory", "Privacy", "Agents"]
    },
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "filed",
        "title": "Hierarchical Conversation Management in Conversational AI Systems",
        "subtitle": "Tree-based branching for structured, focused AI dialogues",
        "venue": "Filed at USPTO",
        "date": "Filed Nov 2025",
        "sortDate": "2025-11-15",
        "summary": "A tree-based conversation architecture that lets users branch from any part of an AI response into isolated child conversations — each inheriting only the relevant context. With interactive mind maps and breadcrumbs for navigation, users can explore topics deeply without polluting the main thread, reducing cognitive load and keeping AI responses sharp and focused.",
        "tags": ["Conversational AI", "UX", "LLM"]
    },
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "filed",
        "title": "Proactive Agent Identification & Suggestion Engine",
        "subtitle": "An AI engine that predicts what you need before you ask",
        "venue": "Filed at USPTO",
        "date": "Filed Oct 2025",
        "sortDate": "2025-10-15",
        "summary": "This innovation automatically understands what the user is trying to do and proactively recommends the most helpful AI agent for the task. Instead of manually hunting for the right tool, it surfaces the perfect assistant in real time — saving effort and making the overall experience seamless across a growing marketplace of agents.",
        "tags": ["Agents", "Recommendation", "Copilot"]
    },
    {
        "type": "patent",
        "status": "filed",
        "title": "Location and Navigation Assistant",
        "subtitle": "AI-powered indoor navigation using multimodal RAG",
        "venue": "Filed at USPTO",
        "date": "Filed Feb 21, 2025",
        "sortDate": "2025-02-21",
        "summary": "An innovative indoor navigation system using multimodal RAG techniques and vision-language models. Users simply take a selfie or point-of-view photo to get location detection and human-friendly, landmark-based navigation directions — no beacons, no floor plans, no manual mapping required.",
        "abstract": "The techniques presented herein are directed to a system for automated navigation assistance in indoor environments. In general, navigating an unfamiliar indoor environment can be a daunting task. This is especially true for large environments such as corporate offices, university campuses, shopping centers, and hospitals. Accordingly, the present system utilizes a reference database containing information associated with the indoor environment such as imagesdepicting various locations, annotations describing the images, and floorplans illustrating the layout of the indoor environment. The reference database is deployed in conjunction with a multimodal language model that utilizes the collected knowledge of the reference database to provide location identification and intuitive navigation instructions. Often referred to as retrieval augmented generation (RAG), the reference database enables the multimodal language model to respond to user requests using domain-specific information and ensure accurate outputs that are relevant to an end user’s context (e.g., the indoor environment)",
        "tags": ["Multimodal RAG", "VLM", "Indoor Navigation"]
    },

    // ------------------------------- PAPERS -------------------------------
    // TODO: Adjust `format` and `archival` for each entry to match reality.
    {
        "type": "paper",
        "format": "Talk",
        "archival": false,
        "title": "AI Builds the UI, Who Judges It? Agent Skills for Visual UX Evaluation of Agentic AI Systems",
        "subtitle": "Turning AI coding assistants into automated visual UX evaluators for agentic AI systems that render dynamic UIs",
        "venue": "Software Engineering Research in India (SERI) 2026 — Industry Track",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-18",
        "summary": "AI agents no longer just reply with text — they now render buttons, cards, forms, and rich widgets right inside their responses (using standards like MCP). But no existing tool can properly check whether that visual output looks and works correctly, especially when every response is dynamically generated. This work turns a general-purpose AI coding assistant (like GitHub Copilot or Claude Code) into an automated UX tester by packaging the evaluation workflow as a reusable 'skill'. The skill-equipped assistant drives real conversations with the agent, takes screenshots at every step, and uses multimodal reasoning to judge each screen against quality rubrics — typography, layout, component integrity, structural completeness — instead of relying on brittle pixel baselines. It carries visual state forward across turns just like a human tester would, handles both chat messages and direct widget interactions (clicks, uploads, toggles), lets teams plug in their own quality checks, and produces reports that go beyond scores by spotting patterns, classifying defects, and attributing root causes. All of it runs inside the developer's IDE — zero infrastructure to spin up.",
        "abstract": "Agentic AI systems are evolving from text responders into interactive content platforms. Protocols like MCP now enable 3P applications (MCP Apps) to render widgets, adaptive cards, and dynamic UI layouts directly within agent responses — composed at runtime from partner templates, LLM-extracted data, and platform rendering logic. Existing tools cannot assess this visual outcome: agent frameworks judge text and tool invocations; visual regression tools require baselines for deterministic UIs; observability platforms trace API calls but never see what the user sees.\n\nWe propose codifying the evaluation workflow as an agent skill — a structured instruction set that turns a general-purpose AI coding assistant (GitHub Copilot, Claude Code) into a specialized UX testing orchestrator. The design models how a human evaluator works: observe visual state changes, validate against quality criteria, rate & reason over the outcome — making the approach inherently tech-stack agnostic. The skill-equipped AI coding assistant navigates live agent platforms via MCP-connected browser workers, captures screenshots of rendered responses, and — leveraging its multimodal capabilities — judges each screenshot against quality rubrics covering typography, layout, component integrity, and structural completeness. Critically, the system handles compounding multi-turn visual state: each turn's non-deterministic visual output becomes the starting state for the next, with variance cascading across turns. The agent captures this cumulative state across both interaction triggers — chat messages and direct widget manipulation (clicks, uploads, toggles) — specified as natural language annotations, eliminating brittle selectors. Beyond scoring, the skill guides the assistant to synthesize cross-query patterns, identify defect classes, attribute root causes (platform-side vs. 3P), and generate reports with narrative insights — tasks requiring integrated visual reasoning only a multimodal AI orchestrator provides.\n\nThis approach combines skill-based orchestration with novel evaluation capabilities: baseline-free visual judgment — the VLM evaluates non-deterministic, dynamically generated content using structured rubrics rather than pixel comparisons; region-scoped multimodal assertions — user-defined checks targeting specific UI regions (widget body, text response, or full canvas) with severity levels, enabling targeted defect analysis; extensible evaluation — stakeholders plug in domain-specific custom rubrics alongside built-in defaults; zero infrastructure overhead — the skill runs in the developer's IDE or CLI and can be distributed via skill marketplaces as a reusable plugin; and composability — the agent reasons through edge cases rather than failing to fixed handlers. We report deployment experience from evaluating MCP Apps — recently generally available in Microsoft 365 Copilot Agents — and discuss reliability trade-offs with mitigations.",
        "tags": ["Agent Evaluation", "Visual UX", "MCP", "Multimodal AI", "Agent Skills"]
    },
    {
        "type": "paper",
        "format": "Poster",
        "archival": false,
        "title": "Who Belongs in the Eval Set? A Capability-Taxonomy-Driven Pipeline for Curating Regression Eval Sets in Agent-Extensibility Platforms",
        "subtitle": "Curating platform-side regression eval sets under a fixed compute and review budget",
        "venue": "Software Engineering Research in India (SERI) 2026 — Industry Track",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-18",
        "summary": "Platform teams that host third-party AI agents (like Copilot's extension ecosystem) face a hard problem: every customer ships their own set of test cases, but the platform can only afford to run so many of them at each release, since each test costs LLM-judge compute and human triage. Admit too much and it becomes unaffordable; reject too much and real regressions slip into production. We built a pipeline that automatically decides which incoming test queries should join the platform's regression set. It maps each query onto a taxonomy of ~30 capabilities the platform cares about (authentication, tool invocation, orchestration, response formats, and so on), then uses a mix of deterministic rules and an LLM classifier to figure out what each query actually exercises. A quality rater compares new queries against existing ones so a sharper test can displace a weaker one that covers the same ground. A rule-based engine issues one of four verdicts — admit, drop, swap, or refer for human review — and eviction always goes through a human, so nothing important disappears silently. We apply the approach to declarative agents with custom actions in Microsoft 365 Copilot, deployed as an MCP server.",
        "abstract": "Platform teams hosting agent-extensibility surfaces face a regression-economics paradox. Customers each ship an eval set, but the platform's living regression set — continuously curated as customers onboard — must stay under a hard query-count ceiling, because each admitted query is LLM-judged per release build and human-triaged on failures. Admitting everything inflates compute, tenant setup, mock data, and reviewer labor, while rejecting too freely opens production blind spots. To the best of our knowledge, no published industrial pipeline addresses this platform-side curation problem. Existing frameworks are customer-side, certifying single agents rather than curating across many incoming sets under a fixed budget.\n\nOur pipeline takes the agent specification and an incoming eval set, projects each query into a platform-owned capability taxonomy of ~30 capabilities (authentication, tool invocation, orchestration, response formats), and emits a per-query decision: admit, drop, swap, or refer for human review. A healthy regression set is the minimal collection of queries that captures the maximal spread of capability signatures, the distinct combinations of capabilities a query exercises together.\n\nA classifier produces a TRUE/FALSE verdict with confidence per (query, capability) pair through two non-overlapping paths. A deterministic specification-based extractor handles capabilities the agent already declares, and is authoritative wherever the specification speaks. A large language model (LLM) with N-sample self-consistency handles capabilities that surface only in query intent.\n\nAn Invocation Quality (IQ) rater addresses what binary coverage cannot. When a new query shares a signature with an existing entry, we ask whether it is a better test, because overlap is common and a shallow tester should yield to a deeper one. The rater scores how thoroughly each query exercises each TRUE capability, so a sharper instance displaces a weaker incumbent.\n\nA consolidator brings coverage and quality together. Given those scores plus facts derived live against the regression set (champions, new coverage, wins, similarity, novelty), a pure rule-based engine emits one of admit, drop, swap, or refer for review. A conservative curator alone writes to the regression set, surfacing eviction candidates but never auto-deleting, because eviction must be auditable.\n\nThree invariants anchor the design. Queries are atomic, so the best test of any single capability is unevictable. Per-capability scores are stored facts about the query, while champion and redundant roles are derived live. Admission is reversible, and eviction is always human-reviewed. The mechanism is taxonomy-agnostic and applies to any eval-set curation problem with a typed capability taxonomy, including ones whose taxonomies evolve as off-distribution queries cluster into extension candidates. We apply it to declarative agents with custom actions in Microsoft 365 Copilot, available as an MCP server.",
        "tags": ["Agent Evaluation", "Eval Curation", "Copilot Extensibility", "MCP"]
    },
    {
        "type": "paper",
        "format": "Poster",
        "archival": false,
        "title": "What Could the Agent See at 19:05? Generating Temporal Enterprise Scenarios from Real Research and Replaying Them to Evaluate Agents",
        "subtitle": "Point-in-time evaluation of enterprise agents via generated temporal scenarios and byte-reproducible replay",
        "venue": "Software Engineering Research in India (SERI) 2026 — Industry Track",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-18",
        "summary": "AI agents today read a person's email, chat, files, and business systems (issue trackers, code repos, incident tools) and act across them. Testing such an agent is currently done against one frozen snapshot of made-up data — but real work is a story that unfolds over hours: a ticket that's 'open' at 10 am is 'closed' by 5 pm, and the correct answer to 'what's the status?' depends entirely on when you ask. So today's evaluations grade the agent as if every story were already over, and every moment in between is skipped. Building a fresh snapshot for each moment is too expensive, and simply dropping 'future' records still leaks tomorrow's information hidden inside a field. Always-on agents that react to changes can't be tested at all. This system fixes both problems. First, it generates believable scenarios grounded in real research — interviews, tickets, incident reports — expanding each into a cast of people, a cause-and-effect storyline, and a timestamped set of records with full ground truth. Then it replays that world at any chosen moment: a fast rule-based pass rebuilds records to how they stood at time T, and a language model only touches the few things rules can't (like a text mention of a later event). Each rebuild is computed once and stored as a tiny diff, so evaluation is instant, reproducible byte-for-byte, and works even for always-on agents. One story becomes dozens of point-in-time evaluation questions, and new apps or skills plug in through a single uniform adapter.",
        "abstract": "A new kind of software now does real knowledge work: AI agents that read a person's email, chat, files, and connected business systems — an issue tracker, a code repository, an incident tool — and act across all of them. Vendors are racing to ship such agents & the add-ons (skills, plugins) that extend them, and how good any is depends on how rigorously it can be evaluated before release. This is where the industry is stuck.\n\nSuch an agent is evaluated today against a sandbox loaded with one frozen snapshot of made-up data. But real work is not a snapshot — it is a story unfolding over hours across many apps, & every moment of that story is a different situation, calling for different questions with different correct answers. Ask 'what is the status of my ticket?' mid-morning and it is 'open'; by day's end it is 'closed' — and the situations in between each invite their own questions. A snapshot is only the last of these, so an agent is graded as if every story were already over, and every in-between moment goes unevaluated. Recreating each moment as its own snapshot — a fresh sandbox per instant — is far too costly. Even within one snapshot, dropping records 'newer than T' still leaks the future hidden inside a record, so the agent looks right while citing facts that did not yet exist. And always-on agents, whose job is to react to a change, cannot be evaluated at all: nothing can place them just after it. Authoring even one realistic multi-app scenario by hand takes days, so teams cannot trust their offline scores and catch regressions only in production.\n\nWe present a system that fixes this with two cooperating capabilities. First, it generates a believable world. Grounded in real research (interviews, tickets, incident reports), it mines recurring situations into scenario seeds, then expands a seed into a cast of people — each with what they may see — a cause-and-effect storyline, a timestamped timeline, and the records those events produce: one self-contained, all-knowing ground truth. Second, it replays that world at any instant. From each app's data schema alone, the system infers how its records evolve, then rebuilds any record exactly as it stood at a chosen moment — a fast rule-based pass for what the clock can decide, and a small, careful use of a language model for what it cannot (a status, or text mentioning later events). Since the evaluable moments are finite and known ahead, every rebuild is computed once and stored as a tiny difference, so evaluation is an instant, exactly-repeatable lookup with no model in the loop — & any agent plugs in through one uniform adapter, judged against machine-generated, moment-specific answers.\n\nOne episode becomes dozens of point-in-time, per-person evals; a new app or skill is onboarded in minutes; batch runs are byte-for-byte reproducible; and even always-on, react-to-change agents become evaluable. We describe the system, its architecture, and the trade-offs of applying it to enterprise agents.",
        "tags": ["Agent Evaluation", "Enterprise Agents", "Temporal Simulation", "Reproducibility"]
    },
    {
